  "live_records": {
    "q-34666": {
      "dom_id": "step-q34666",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the curve  $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+(\\sqrt{2}t-2)\\,j-t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=2t$, find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, since $s=2t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{s}{2}+2)i+(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{2}-2)j-\\frac{s}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =(\\frac{s+4}{2})i+(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s-4}{2})j-\\frac{s}{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s+4}{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}s-4}{2})j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j-\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
