    "q-127732": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127732",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,1-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3u\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s=3t,$ we have that $t=\\frac{s}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})-2,\\,1-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
